Six-week project plan
Week 1
Research and collect clinical trial data
Find historical Phase II therapeutic cancer vaccine trials across different cancer types. Record the information in your selected table: trial design, efficacy, statistical significance, safety, patient characteristics and Phase III progression.
Deliverable: A spreadsheet containing an initial 10–20 vaccine programmes to check data availability, with links to every source.

Week 2 
Clean the dataset and explore relationships
Convert the research into a structured dataset, address missing values and identify which historical trials progressed to Phase III.
Use Python to explore questions such as: Do vaccines with stronger efficacy results progress more often? Does trial size matter? Are severe adverse events associated with lower progression rates?
Deliverable: A clean dataset, descriptive statistics and exploratory graphs.

Week 3
Build and evaluate the prediction model
Train a logistic regression model to predict the probability of Phase III progression using Phase II information. Compare it with a more complex model only if your sample size supports that.
Test how well the model predicts historical outcomes and whether its predicted probabilities are reliable.
Deliverable: A model producing a probability such as 60% likelihood of entering Phase III, with an honest assessment of reliability.

Week 4
Apply the model to melanoma
Select a melanoma therapeutic vaccine, such as V940 (mRNA-4157), and collect its Phase II data.
Feed the relevant inputs into your model to estimate whether it would have predicted advancement to Phase III. Compare this prediction against the actual historical development decision.
Deliverable: A melanoma case study combining clinical evidence, model prediction and interpretation.

Week 5
Build the pharmaceutical valuation model
Estimate the vaccine's potential revenue, future operating cash flows, development expenses and remaining probability of approval. Combine these with your Phase III progression probability using risk-adjusted net present value (rNPV).
Deliverable: A financial model estimating the value of the vaccine programme under different scenarios.

Week 6 
Evaluate and present your findings
Bring together your predictive model and valuation. Explain the variables that appear most useful, model limitations, uncertainty and how a pharmaceutical investor might interpret the results.
Deliverable: Final presentation, reproducible analysis and a clear conclusion to the research question.


